### TITLE ###
StateCarriesBetweenTurns
### DESCRIPTION ###
The point of a session: one interpreter, one environment, many turns. Note
assignments print nothing and expressions print their value, so the output is
the result of what you asked for and nothing else.
### ARGS ###
repl
### STDIN ###
x = 10
y = 32
x + y
x = x * 2
x
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDOUT ###
🤙 Rad REPL v0.11.0
Type ':help' for help, ':exit' or Ctrl+D to quit.

42
20

### TITLE ###
ValuesPrintAsTheyWouldBeWritten
### DESCRIPTION ###
Strings come back quoted so you can tell "5" from 5. This is repr, not print -
print() is still available and still unquoted.
### ARGS ###
repl
### STDIN ###
"hello"
[1, 2, 3]
{"a": 1}
1 == 1
print("unquoted")
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDOUT ###
🤙 Rad REPL v0.11.0
Type ':help' for help, ':exit' or Ctrl+D to quit.

"hello"
[ 1, 2, 3 ]
{ "a": 1 }
true
unquoted

### TITLE ###
ExitEndsTheSessionWithItsCode
### DESCRIPTION ###
exit() is the ordinary builtin, not a magic word the reader intercepts, so it
takes an exit code like it does anywhere else.
### ARGS ###
repl
### STDIN ###
print("before")
exit(3)
print("never runs")
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDOUT ###
🤙 Rad REPL v0.11.0
Type ':help' for help, ':exit' or Ctrl+D to quit.

before
### EXIT ###
3

### TITLE ###
EmptyLinesAreSkipped
### DESCRIPTION ###
A blank line at the top level is not an empty turn to evaluate, it is nothing.
### ARGS ###
repl
### STDIN ###
x = 1


x
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDOUT ###
🤙 Rad REPL v0.11.0
Type ':help' for help, ':exit' or Ctrl+D to quit.

1
